                = succeed ";" do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %li
              %p
                the
                %strong twistor space
                is the
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+projective+3-space"} complex projective 3-space
                = succeed ";" do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %mi ℂ
                        %msup
                          %mi P
                          %mn 3
                        %mo =
                        %msub
                          %mi Gr
                          %mn 1
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                        %msup
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mn 4
                        %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^3 = Gr_1(\mathbb{C}^4)
            %li
              %p
                the correspondence space
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi Gr
                        %mrow
                          %mn 1
                          %mo ,
                          %mn 2
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi ℂ
                        %mn 4
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gr_{1, 2}(\mathbb{C}^4)
                is the space of lines in planes in
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msup
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mn 4
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^4
          %p
            (e.g.
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#WardWells90"} Ward-Wells 90
          %h2#details Details
          %h3#TwistorsFor4dMinkowskiSpacetime Twistors for 4d Minkowski space
          %p
            We discuss the original twistors for the description of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/physics"} physics
            in 4d
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            vector and an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angular+momentum"} angular momentum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            subject to the constraint that the momentum is
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            and of definite
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/helicity"} helicity
          %p
            The formulation is all motivated from the form that basic quantities of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/special+relativity"} special relativistic physics
            take when
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/vectors"} vectors
            are expressed in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            coordinates via the
            %a{:href => "spin+group#ExceptionalIsomorphisms"} exceptional spin isomorphism
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Spin
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 1
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≃
                  %mi SL
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ℂ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Spin(3,1) \simeq SL(2,\mathbb{C})
                  \\,.
          %p
            Under this identification a chiral
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi κ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \kappa
            is represented just by a pair of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ξ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi η
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \xi, \eta \in \mathbb{C}
            and one may write the spinor as
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %msup
                      %mi κ
                      %mi α
                    %mo )
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi ξ
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mi η
                    %mo )
                  %mo ∈
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left(
                  \\kappa^\alpha
                  \\right)
                  \=
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  \\xi
                  \\\
                  \\eta
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\in
                  \\;\; \mathbb{C}^2
                  \\,.
          %p Moreover, via the non-degenerate bilinear pairing
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Γ
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⊗
                  %mi S
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi V
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\Gamma \;\colon\; S \otimes S \longrightarrow V
          %p
            given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
            (see at
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+representation"} spin representation
            for details) one can express vectors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 0
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 1
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 2
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mn 3
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (x^i) = (x^0, x^1, x^2, x^3)
          %p
            in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            as “bi-spinors” given by 2x2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitean+matrices"} Hermitean matrices
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mrow
                                %mi α
                                %mi β
                            %mo )
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %msup
                            %mi x
                            %mi i
                          %msubsup
                            %mi γ
                            %mi i
                            %mrow
                              %mi α
                              %mi β
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                            %mfrac
                              %mn 1
                              %msqrt
                                %mn 2
                          %mrow
                            %mo (
                            %mrow
                              %mtable
                                %mtr
                                  %mtd
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 0
                                    %mo +
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 3
                                  %mtd
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 1
                                    %mo +
                                    %mi i
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 2
                                %mtr
                                  %mtd
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 1
                                    %mo −
                                    %mi i
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 2
                                  %mtd
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 0
                                    %mo −
                                    %msup
                                      %mi x
                                      %mn 3
                            %mo )
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                          %mo ∈
                          %msub
                            %mi Mat
                            %mn 2
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                          %mi ℂ
                          %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  \\left(x^{\alpha \beta}\right)
                  \&amp; \coloneqq
                  (x^i \gamma_i^{\alpha \beta})
                  \\\
                  \&amp;
                  \=
                  \\tfrac{1}{\sqrt{2}}
                  \\left(
                  \\array{
                  x^0 + x^3 &amp; x^1 + i x^2
                  \\\
                  x^1 - i x^2 &amp; x^0 - x^3
                  }
                  \\right)
                  \\;\;
                  \\in Mat_{2}(\mathbb{C})
                  \\end{aligned}
                  \\,,
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi γ
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \gamma_i
            denote the generators of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Clifford+algebra"} Clifford algebra
            given by the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+matrices"} Pauli matrices
          %p
            This is such that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lorentz+metric"} Lorentz metric
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            is just the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
            of this
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrix"} matrix
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ‖
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi x
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:maxsize => "1.2em", :minsize => "1.2em"} ‖
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mn 2
                  %mi det
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi x
                        %mrow
                          %mi α
                          %mi β
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\big\Vert (x^i) \big\Vert
                  \\;=\;
                  2 det\left(\left(x^{\alpha \beta} \right)\right)
                  \\,.
          %p
            This spinorial re-expression of vectors turns out to yield very efficient expressions particularly for those kinds of terms that appear in the observed physics of massless chiral particles, such as they appear in the
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/standard+model+of+particle+physics"} standard model of particle physics
          %p
            For instance a skew rank-2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            (2-from)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (F_{i j})
            with
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %mi j
                      %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F_{i j} = - F_{j i}
            (for instance the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+strength"} field strength
            of an
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/electromagnetic+field"} electromagnetic field
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mtable{:columnalign => "right left right left right left right left right left", :columnspacing => "0em", :displaystyle => "true"}
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 2
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 2
                        %mtd
                          %mo ≔
                          %msub
                            %mi F
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 2
                          %msubsup
                            %mi γ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 1
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 1
                          %msubsup
                            %mi γ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 2
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 2
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi i
                                %mn 2
                      %mtr
                        %mtd
                        %mtd
                          %mo =
                          %msub
                            %mi ϕ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 2
                          %msub
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 2
                          %mo +
                          %msub
                            %mi ψ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi β
                                %mn 2
                          %msub
                            %mi ϵ
                            %mrow
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 1
                              %msub
                                %mi α
                                %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\begin{aligned}
                  F_{\alpha_1 \beta_1 \alpha_2 \beta_2}
                  \&amp; \coloneqq
                  F_{i_1 i_2} \gamma^{i_1}_{\alpha_1 \beta_1} \gamma^{i_2}_{\alpha_2 \beta_2}
                  \\\
                  \&amp; =
                  \\phi_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2} \epsilon_{\beta_1 \beta_2}
                  +
                  \\psi_{\beta_1 \beta_2} \epsilon_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}
                  \\end{aligned}
          %p
            for complex numbers
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\phi_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2})
            and
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi β
                          %mn 1
                        %msub
                          %mi β
                          %mn 2
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\psi_{\beta_1 \beta_2})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi F
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} F
            itself is real-number valued, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+conjugate"} complex conjugate
            of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            and hence any real 2-form is encoded equivalently by a bispinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ϕ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \phi
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi F
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 2
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi ϕ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  F_{\alpha_1 \beta_1 \alpha_2 \beta_2}
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\phi_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2} \epsilon_{\beta_1 \beta_2}
                  +
                  \\overline{\phi}_{\beta_1 \beta_2} \epsilon_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}
          %p
            Crucially the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hodge+star"} Hodge dual
            of a 2-form has then the simple expression
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi F
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 2
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 2
                  %mo +
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi β
                        %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mi ϵ
                    %mrow
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 1
                      %msub
                        %mi α
                        %mn 2
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\star F)_{\alpha_1 \beta_1 \alpha_2 \beta_2}
                  \=
                  \- i \phi_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2} \epsilon_{\beta_1 \beta_2}
                  +
                  i \psi_{\beta_1 \beta_2} \epsilon_{\alpha_1 \alpha_2}
                  \\,,
          %p
            which is the first sign that
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+Yang-Mills+theory"} self-dual Yang-Mills theory
            has a simpler expression in terms of such spinorial coordinates.
          %p
            Now characterizing an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/elementary+particle"} elementary particle
            is (by the discussion at
            = succeed ")" do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/unitary+representation+of+the+Poincar%C3%A9+group"} unitary representation of the Poincaré group
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            4-vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p^i)
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angular+momentum"} angular momentum
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/tensor"} tensor
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi M
                      %mrow
                        %mi i
                        %mi j
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (M^{i j})
            %strong twistor
            is effectively a pair of spinorial coordinates expression this data for
            %em massless
            and
            %em chiral
            particles.
          %p
            Here chiral means this: from combining the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/momentum"} momentum
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/angular+momentum"} angular momentum
            one obtains the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli-Lubanski+vector"} Pauli-Lubanski vector
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %mo ≔
                  %mstyle{:displaystyle => "false"}
                    %mfrac
                      %mn 1
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mo ⋆
                  %mi M
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_i \coloneqq \tfrac{1}{2} (\star M)_{i j} p^j
          %p and chiral particles satisfy
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi S
                    %mi i
                  %mo =
                  %mi s
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  S_i  = s p_i
          %p
            for some constant
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi s
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} s
          %p
            A
            %em twistor
            is a set of spinorial coordinates for encoding tensors
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                      %mo )
                    %mo ,
                    %mrow
                      %mo (
                      %msup
                        %mi M
                        %mrow
                          %mi i
                          %mi j
                      %mo )
                    %mo )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \left(\left(p^i\right), \left(M^{i j}\right)\right)
            which satisfy
          %ol
            %li
              %p
                masslessness:
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %msup
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                      %msub
                        %mi p
                        %mi i
                      %mo =
                      %mn 0
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p^i p_i = 0
            %li
              %p
                chirality
                = succeed "." do
                  %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                    %semantics
                      %mrow
                        %msub
                          %mi S
                          %mi i
                        %mo =
                        %mi s
                        %msub
                          %mi p
                          %mi i
                      %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} S_i = s p_i
          %p
            By the discussion at
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/celestial+sphere"} celestial sphere
            we have that the first condition means equivalently that there is a single
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\pi_\alpha)
            such that the spinorial expression for the momentum is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi p
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mi β
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi π
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi π
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi β
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  p_{\alpha \beta} = \pi_\alpha \overline{\pi}_\beta
                  \\,.
          %p
            By the above discussion of 2-forms we know moreover that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} M^{i j}
            is encoded by a bispinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mi β
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mu_{\alpha \beta}
            and imposing the chirality constraint one finds, using the above formula for the Hodge dual, that its solutions are parameterized precisely by another spinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\omega_\alpha)
            via
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi μ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mi β
                  %mo =
                  %mi i
                  %msub
                    %mi ω
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi π
                      %mo ¯
                    %mrow
                      %mi β
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\overline{\mu}_{\alpha \beta} = i \omega_{(\alpha} \overline{\pi}_{\beta)}
          %p (where the parenthesis denote symmetrization of indices).
          %p
            In summary, the possible momentum and angular momentum
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi p
                    %mi i
                  %mo ,
                  %msup
                    %mi M
                    %mrow
                      %mi i
                      %mi j
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (p^i, M^{i j})
            of massless chiral particles in 4d Minkowski spacetime is parameterized precisely by pair of spinors
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo =
                  %mrow
                    %mo (
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ,
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msub
                      %mi π
                      %mi β
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 4
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z =
                  \\left(
                  (\omega^\alpha), (\pi_\beta)
                  \\right)
                  \\;\;
                  \\in
                  \\mathbb{C}^4
                  \\,.
          %p
            The image of this under modding out by a global complex factor is a Penrose
            %em twistor
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi Z
                  %mo ∈
                  %mi ℂ
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 3
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z \in \mathbb{C}P^3
                  \\,,
          %p
            an element of the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+projective+3-space"} complex projective 3-space
            = succeed "." do
              %em twistor space
          %h3#TwistorSpace Twistor space
          %p
            To discuss twistor space for Minkowski spacetime, it is useful to work more generally with
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 4
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 6
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 10
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \{3,4,6,10\}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spin+representation"} spin representation
            then by the discussion
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "spin+representation#InTermsOfNormedDivisionAlgebraInDimension3To10"} there
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi ℍ
                    %mo ,
                    %mi 𝕆
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K} \in \{\mathbb{R}, \mathbb{C}, \mathbb{H}, \mathbb{O}\}
            = succeed "," do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+division+algebra"} supersymmetry and division algebra
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#BengtssonCederwall88"} Bengtsson-Cederwall 88
          %p
            With this, the vector space underlying
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
            is identified with the space of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hermitian+matrices"} hermitian matrices
            with entries in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi linear
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %mi hermitian
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1} \simeq_{linear} Mat_{2 \times 2}^{hermitian}(\mathbb{K})
          %p
            and the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+metric"} Minkowski metric
            = succeed "-square" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/norm"} norm
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/determinant"} determinant
            of matrices:
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi η
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo ,
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mi det
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi v
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi v
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %msub
                    %mo ≃
                    %mi linear
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %mi hermitian
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\eta(v,v) = det(v)  \;\;\; v\in \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1} \simeq_{linear} Mat_{2 \times 2}^{hermitian}(\mathbb{K})
                  \\,.
          %p
            A choice of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/linear+basis"} linear basis
            for this space is provided by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Pauli+matrices"} Pauli matrices
            with coefficients in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 0
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %mn 1
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %mn 1
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo ,
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo +
                      %mn 1
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi n
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mi e
                                %mo ¯
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\Gamma_0^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\left[
                  \\array{
                  1 &amp; 0
                  \\\
                  0 &amp; -1
                  }
                  \\right]
                  \\;\;\,,
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  (\Gamma_1^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\left[
                  \\array{
                  1 &amp; 0
                  \\\
                  0 &amp; 1
                  }
                  \\right]
                  \\;\;\,,
                  \\;\;\;\;
                  (\Gamma_{n+1}^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\left[
                  \\array{
                  0 &amp; e_n
                  \\\
                  \\overline{e}_n &amp; 0
                  }
                  \\right]
          %p
            where
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %msub
                    %mi e
                    %mi n
                  %msubsup
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mi n
                      %mo =
                      %mn 2
                    %mi d
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \{e_n\}_{n = 2}^{d}
            denotes an
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/orthonormal+basis"} orthonormal basis
            for the elements of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mo ¯
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace"} :
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo ⟶
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\overline{(-)}
                  \\;\colon\;
                  \\mathbb{K}
                  \\longrightarrow
                  \\mathbb{K}
          %p
            for the operation of complex conjugation in
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            = succeed "-linear" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 1
            to itself, and sends imaginary elements to their negative.
          %p
            In terms of this basis then a
            = succeed "-component" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi μ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^\mu) \in \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
            is identified with the hermitian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            matrix
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo ≔
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mi μ
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Γ
                    %mi μ
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mo =
                  %mspace{:width => "thickmathspace"}
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mn 0
                            %mo +
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mn 1
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mi n
                            %msub
                              %mi e
                              %mi n
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mi n
                            %msub
                              %mover
                                %mi e
                                %mo ¯
                              %mi n
                          %mtd
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mn 0
                            %mo −
                            %msup
                              %mi x
                              %mn 1
                    %mo ]
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (X^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
                  \\;\coloneqq\;
                  (X^\mu \Gamma_\mu^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
                  \\;=\;
                  \\left[
                  \\array{
                  x^0 + x^1 &amp; x^{n} e_n
                  \\\
                  x^n \overline{e}_n &amp; x^0 - x^1
                  }
                  \\right]
                  \\,.
          %p
            This presentation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Minkowski+spacetime"} Minkowski spacetime
            by
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            hermitian matrices with coefficients in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            makes it very natural to consider also its “
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}>
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
            \-ification” (hence
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complexification"} complexification
            in the case that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 4
            and
            = succeed ")," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mo =
                    %mi ℂ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K} = \mathbb{C}
            %em all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/matrices"} matrices
            with entries in
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{K}\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
                  \\coloneqq
                  Mat_{2\times 2}(\mathbb{K})
                  \\,.
          %p
            This space carries the operation of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Hermitian+conjugation"} Hermitian conjugation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                  %mo ↦
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mo †
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X \mapsto X^\dagger
          %p
            as an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/involution"} involution
            = succeed "-dimensional" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/fixed+point"} fixed point
            set of this involution.
          %p
            By the discussion
            = succeed "," do
              %a{:href => "spin+representation#InTermsOfNormedDivisionAlgebraInDimension3To10"} there
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ,
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi, \phi \in \mathbb{K}^2
            to a vector
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mover
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo ∈
                  %msubsup
                    %mi Mat
                    %mrow
                      %mn 2
                      %mo ×
                      %mn 2
                    %mi hermitian
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \overline{\psi} \Gamma \phi \in Mat_{2\times 2}^{hermitian}(\mathbb{K})
            is given by (
            %a{:href => "spin+representation#RealSpinorPairingsViaDivisionAlg"}> prop.
            )
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mover
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo ¯
                  %mi Γ
                  %mi ϕ
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %mi ψ
                  %msup
                    %mi ϕ
                    %mo †
                  %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} +
                  %mi ϕ
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo †
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\overline{\psi}\Gamma \phi)
                  \\coloneqq
                  \\psi \phi^\dagger + \phi \psi^\dagger
                  \\,.
          %p
            In particular the pairing of a spinor
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi = (\psi_{\dot \alpha})
            with itself is
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi ψ
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mo †
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                    %mrow
                      %mi α
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                  %mo =
                  %mn 2
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  (\psi \psi^\dagger)_{\alpha \dot\alpha}
                  \=
                  2
                  \\overline{\psi}_\alpha \psi_{\dot \alpha}
                  \\,.
          %p
            Since, as hermitian matrices, the
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            matrices arising this way are manifestly precisely the hermitian projection operators on 1-dimensional linear subspaces of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi 𝕂
                      %mn 2
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}^2
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2 \times 2
            hermitian matrices with vanishing determinant. Hence the future-oriented
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/lightlike"} lightlike
            vectors in Minkowski spacetime in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mn 3
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 4
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 6
                  %mo ,
                  %mn 10
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = \{3,4,6,10\}
            are precisely those that arise as the pairing of a chiral spinor with itself.
          %p
            The above formula shows that as we rescale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mi k
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi \mapsto k \psi
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{K}
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mover
                      %mi k
                      %mo ¯
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \overline{k} \in \mathbb{R}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projective space
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}P^1
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %mo =
                  %mi ℂ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K} = \mathbb{C}
            (the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/complex+numbers"} complex numbers
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/celestial+sphere"} celestial sphere
            (in this case: the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Riemann+sphere"} Riemann sphere
            = succeed ")." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ℂ
                    %msup
                      %mi P
                      %mn 1
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}P^1
          %p
            Lightlike vectors in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
            up to scale are equivalently loci of lightlike
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geodesics"} geodesics
            through the origin. A general lightlike geodesic in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
            is instead parameterized by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi τ
                  %mo ↦
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %mi τ
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi α
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\tau \mapsto (X^{\dot \alpha \alpha} + \tau {\psi}^\alpha \overline{\psi}^{\dot \alpha})
          %p
            for some offset
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi α
                          %mo ˙
                        %mi α
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            under the Clifford action with
            = succeed ":" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi X
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi α
                  %mo ≔
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega^\alpha \coloneqq \psi_{\dot \alpha} X^{\dot \alpha \alpha}
                  \\,.
          %p
            This relation bewteen
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            is called the
            %em incidence relation
            in this context.
          %p
            The locus of the lightlike geodesic does not change as we rescale
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ψ
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi
            by elements
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi k
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} k \in \mathbb{K}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ω
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega
            gets rescaled accordingly. Hence the pair
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\omega^\alpha, \psi_{\dot \alpha})
            regarded as encoding lightlike geodesics in Minkowski spacetime should be thought of as an element of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/projective+space"} projective space
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 3
                  %mo ≔
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi Z
                    %mi a
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ∈
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mn 4
                  %msub
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                    %mrow
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %msup
                        %mi Z
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo ∼
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                      %mi k
                      %msup
                        %mi Z
                        %mi a
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                      %mi k
                      %mo ∈
                      %mi 𝕂
                      %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{K}P^3
                  \\coloneqq \{ (Z^{a}) = (\omega^\alpha,\psi_{\dot \alpha}) \in \mathbb{K}^4 \}_{( (Z^a) \sim (k Z^a) \vert k \in \mathbb{K} )}.
          %p
            This is the
            %em twistor space
            corresponding to the given Minkowski spacetime.
          %p
            However, by the above definition of
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega^\alpha
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi d
                    %mo ∈
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} {
                    %mn 3
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 4
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 6
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} }
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d \in \{3,4,6\}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi α
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi α
                  %mo =
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %msup
                    %mover
                      %mi ω
                      %mo ¯
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\omega^\alpha \overline{\psi}_\alpha
                  \=
                  \\psi_{\dot \alpha} \overline{\omega}^{\dot \alpha}
          %p
            reflecting the fact that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (X^{\dot \alpha \alpha})
            is a hermitian matrix, instead of a general
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mn 2
                  %mo ×
                  %mn 2
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} 2\times 2
            matrix. (For
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi d
                  %mo =
                  %mn 10
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} d = 10
            there is no elegant statement like this, due to the non-associativity of the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/octonions"} octonions
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mo =
                    %mi 𝕆
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K} = \mathbb{O}
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#Witten86"} Witten 86
          %p Hence the subspace inclusion
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %msup
                    %mi ℝ
                    %mrow
                      %mi d
                      %mo −
                      %mn 1
                      %mo ,
                      %mn 1
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{K}\mathbb{R}^{d-1,1}
          %p
            of Minkowski-spacetime into its
            = succeed "-ification," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕂
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\mathcal{N} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{K}P^3
          %p
            of twistor space. This is called the space of
            = succeed "," do
              %em null twistors
            = succeed "." do
              %em spin shell
          %p
            If we consider the metric on
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi 𝕂
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{K}^4
            given in component by
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mrow
                    %mo [
                    %mrow
                      %mtable
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                          %mtd
                            %msubsup
                              %mi δ
                              %mi α
                              %mi β
                        %mtr
                          %mtd
                            %mo{:lspace => "verythinmathspace", :rspace => "0em"} −
                            %msubsup
                              %mi δ
                              %mover
                                %mi β
                                %mo ˙
                              %mover
                                %mi α
                                %mo ˙
                          %mtd
                            %mn 0
                    %mo ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  \\left[
                  \\array{
                  0 &amp; \delta^\beta_\alpha
                  \\\
                  \- \delta^{\dot \alpha}_{\dot \beta} &amp; 0
                  }
                  \\right]
          %p
            then this subspace is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadric"} quadric
            given by the equation
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Z
                    %mi a
                  %msub
                    %mover
                      %mi Z
                      %mo ¯
                    %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mn 0
                  %mspace{:width => "thinmathspace"}
                  %mo .
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  Z^a \overline{Z}_a = 0
                  \\,.
          %p
            Now conversely, given
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi Z
                    %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ω
                    %mi α
                  %mo ,
                  %msub
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Z^a = (\omega^\alpha, \psi_{\dot\alpha})
            not equal to zero and satisfying this condition in that there is a hermitian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X^{\dot \alpha \alpha}
            with
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %msup
                      %mi ω
                      %mi α
                    %mo =
                    %msub
                      %mi ψ
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                    %msup
                      %mi X
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi α
                          %mo ˙
                        %mi α
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \omega^{\alpha} = \psi_{\dot \alpha} X^{\dot \alpha \alpha}
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi X
                    %mrow
                      %mover
                        %mi α
                        %mo ˙
                      %mi α
                  %mo +
                  %mi τ
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mover
                      %mi α
                      %mo ˙
                  %msup
                    %mi ψ
                    %mi α
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"}
                  X^{\dot \alpha \alpha} + \tau \psi^{\dot \alpha}\psi^\alpha
          %p
            (for
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi τ
                    %mo ∈
                    %mi ℝ
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \tau \in \mathbb{R}
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi X
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} X
            in the direction of
            = succeed ")" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi ψ
                    %msup
                      %mi ψ
                      %mo †
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \psi \psi^\dagger
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %msup
                      %mi ϵ
                      %mrow
                        %mover
                          %mi α
                          %mo ˙
                        %mover
                          %mi β
                          %mo ˙
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\epsilon^{\dot \alpha \dot \beta})
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                  %mo ↪
                  %mi 𝕂
                  %msup
                    %mi P
                    %mn 3
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N} \hookrightarrow \mathbb{K}P^3
            correspond to lightlike geodesics in Minkowski spacetime.
          %p
            %img{:src => "http://ncatlab.org/nlab/files/TwistorSpaceCorrespondence.jpg", :width => "700"}/
          %p
            (graphics from
            = succeed ")" do
              %a{:href => "#Adamo13"} Adamo 13
          %p
            This correspondence extends also to the 0-point in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi 𝒩
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathcal{N}
            if one passes to “compactified Minkowski spacetime”, given by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Grassmannian"} Grassmannian
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi Gr
                    %mn 2
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 4
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Gr_2(\mathbb{C}^4)
            of complex planes in
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msup
                    %mi ℂ
                    %mn 4
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{C}^4
            (review includes
            = succeed ")." do
              %a{:href => "#FioresiLledoVaradarajan07"} Fioresi-Lledo-Varadarajan 07, section 2
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+fibration"} twistor fibration
            %li
              %p
                A real analog of this is the
                = succeed "." do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+transform"} Radon transform
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/spinor"} spinor
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+string+theory"} twistor string theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MHV+amplitude"} MHV amplitude
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Radon+transform"} Radon transform
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Schubert+calculus"} Schubert calculus
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/celestial+sphere"}> celestial sphere
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quadric"} quadric
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Klein+quadric"} Klein quadric
          %h2#references References
          %h3#general General
          %p The notion originates in
          %ul
            %li#Penrose67
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"}> Roger Penrose
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twistor algebra
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1063/1.1705200"} doi:10.1063/1.1705200
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"}> Roger Penrose
                ,
                %em The twistor programme, Reports on Mathematical Physics 12 (1): 65–76 (1977)
          %p
            Key developments concerning
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/scattering+amplitudes"} scattering amplitudes
            are due to
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andrew+Hodges"}> Andrew Hodges
              ,
              %em Twistor diagrams
              (
              %a{:href => "http://www.twistordiagrams.org.uk/papers/index.html"}> web
              )
          %p
            The relation to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+Yang-Mills+theory"} self-dual Yang-Mills theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Ward77
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+S.+Ward"}> Richard S. Ward
              \:
              = succeed "," do
                %em On Selfdual gauge fields
              %strong 61
              (1977) 81-82 &amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0375-9601(77)90842-8"}> doi:10.1016/0375-9601(77)90842-8
              \&amp;rbrack;
          %p with comprehensive discussion in:
          %ul
            %li#WardWells90
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Richard+S.+Ward"}> Richard S. Ward
              , R. O. Wells,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twistor geometry and field theory
              = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/CBO9780511524493"} doi:10.1017/CBO9780511524493
          %p Introductions and surveys:
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yuri+Manin"}> Yuri Manin
                , chapters 1 and 2 of
                %em
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gauge+Field+Theory+and+Complex+Geometry"}> Gauge Field Theory and Complex Geometry
                  , Grundlehren der Mathematischen Wissenschaften 289, Springer 1988
            %li
              %p
                Fedja Hadrovich,
                %em Twistor primer
                (
                %a{:href => "http://users.ox.ac.uk/~tweb/00004"}> html
                ,
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://henry.pha.jhu.edu/twistors.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                Paul Bair,
                %em Introduction to twistors
                (
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.jussieu.fr/~helein/encyclopaedia/baird-twistors.pdf"}> pdf
                )
            %li
              %p
                S. A. Huggett, K. P. Todd,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em An introduction to twistor theory
            %li
              %p
                Liana David,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Penrose transform and its applications
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://www.maths.ed.ac.uk/pg/thesis/david.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Dunajski09
              %p
                Maciej Dunajski,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twistor theory and differential equations
                = succeed ":404004" do
                  %strong A42
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/0902.0274"} arXiv:0902.0274
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1088/1751-8113/42/40/404004"} doi
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"}> Roger Penrose
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twistor theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/New+Spaces+for+Mathematics+and+Physics"} New Spaces for Mathematics and Physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=kmYfYOW0vSg"} video recording
            %li#FioresiLledoVaradarajan07
              %p
                R. Fioresi, M. A. Lledo,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Veeravalli+Varadarajan"} Veeravalli Varadarajan
                = succeed "," do
                  %em The Minkowski and conformal superspaces
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/math/0609813"} arXiv:0609813
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+Atiyah"}> Michael Atiyah
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Maciej+Dunajski"} Maciej Dunajski
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lionel+J.+Mason"} Lionel J. Mason
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Twistor theory at fifty: from contour integrals to twistor strings
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1704.07464"} arXiv:1704.07464
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1098/rspa.2017.0530"} doi:10.1098/rspa.2017.0530
            %li#Adamo17
              %p
                Tim Adamo,
                %em Lectures on twistor theory
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1712.02196"}> arXiv:1712.02196
                )
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lionel+J.+Mason"}> Lionel J. Mason
                , N. M. J. Woodhouse,
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Integrability, self-duality and twistor theory
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Roger+Penrose"}> Roger Penrose
                ,
                %em Twistor Theory: A Geometric Perspective for Describing the Physical World
                (
                %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108854399.011"}> doi:10.1017/9781108854399.011
                ), chapter in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Gabriel+Catren"} Gabriel Catren
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Mathieu+Anel"} Mathieu Anel
                (eds.)
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/New+Spaces+for+Mathematics+and+Physics"} New Spaces for Mathematics and Physics
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108854429"} doi:10.1017/9781108854429
          %p
            Review of the application in
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/super+Yang-Mills+theory"} super Yang-Mills theory
            includes
          %ul
            %li#Adamo13
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Tim+Adamo"}> Tim Adamo
              ,
              %em Twistor actions for gauge theory and gravity
              (
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1308.2820"}> arXiv:1308.2820
              )
          %p See also
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "http://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Twistor_theory"} Twistor theory
          %h3#relation_to_geometric_representation_theory_and_parabolic_geometry Relation to geometric representation theory and parabolic geometry
          %p
            A discussion in the general context of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/geometric+representation+theory"} geometric representation theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li#BastonEastwood89
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Robert+J.+Baston"}> Robert J. Baston
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Michael+G.+Eastwood"} Michael G. Eastwood
              %em The Penrose transform its interaction with representation theory
              Oxford Science Publications, Clarendon Press, 1989 (ISBN:9780198535652,
              = succeed "," do
                %a{:href => "https://inspirehep.net/literature/290342"} spire:290342
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://www.math.utah.edu/~ptrapa/math-library/baston-eastwood/baston-eastwood.pdf"} pdf
          %p
            and the further generalization to
            = succeed "/" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cartan+geometry"} Cartan geometry
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/parabolic+geometry"} parabolic geometry
            is discussed in
          %ul
            %li#Cap01
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Andreas+%C4%8Cap"}> Andreas Čap
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Correspondence spaces and twistor spaces for parabolic geometries
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/math/0102097"} arXiv:math/0102097
          %h3#palatial_twistor_theory Palatial twistor theory
          %ul
            %li
              Roger Penrose,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Palatial twistor theory and the twistor googly problem
              %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1098/rsta.2014.0237"} doi
            %li
              R. Penrose,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twistor theory as an approach to fundamental physics
              %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1007/978-3-319-64813-2_10"} doi
          %h3#application_to_quantum_field_theory Application to quantum field theory
          %p
            More on traditional applications to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/quantum+field+theory"} quantum field theory
            is in
          %ul
            %li
              %a{:href => "#WardWells90"} Ward &amp; Wells 1990
          %p
            The relation of twistor geometry to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/MHV+amplitudes"} MHV amplitudes
            in 4d
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Yang-Mills+theory"} Yang-Mills theory
            and
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/twistor+string+theory"} twistor string theory
            is due to
          %ul
            %li#Witten03
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Perturbative Gauge Theory As A String Theory In Twistor Space
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/0312171"} arXiv:hep-th/0312171
          %p Surveys of the resulting modern application of twistors in field theory include
          %ul
            %li
              David Skinner,
              = succeed "," do
                %em The geometry of scattering amplitudes
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://research.physics.unc.edu/string/transparencies/trans_20091112.pdf"} pdf
          %h3#twistors_in_higher_dimensions Twistors in higher dimensions
          %p
            Discussion of twistors in dimensions 3,4,6 and 10 using the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/normed+division+algebras"} normed division algebras
            (as in
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/supersymmetry+and+division+algebras"} supersymmetry and division algebras
          %ul
            %li#BengtssonCederwall88
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Ingemar+Bengtsson"}> Ingemar Bengtsson
                ,
                = succeed "," do
                  %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Cederwall"} Martin Cederwall
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Particles, Twistors and the Division Algebras
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "http://inspirehep.net/record/247269"} spire:247269
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(88)90667-0"} doi:10.1016/0550-3213(88)90667-0
            %li#Cederwall93
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Cederwall"}> Martin Cederwall
                ,
                %em Introduction to Division Algebras, Sphere Algebras and Twistors
                (
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/hep-th/9310115"}> arXiv:hep-th/9310115
                )
          %p and specifically for dimension 10
          %ul
            %li#Witten86
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Edward+Witten"}> Edward Witten
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twistor-like transform in ten dimensions
              = succeed ":2," do
                %strong B266
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://dx.doi.org/10.1016/0550-3213(86)90090-8"} 10.1016/0550-3213(86)90090-8
          %p
            and its
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/anti+de+Sitter+spacetime"} AdS
            version:
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Alex+Arvanitakis"}> Alex Arvanitakis
              , Alec E. Barns-Graham,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Paul+Townsend"} Paul Townsend
              = succeed "," do
                %em Twistor description of spinning particles in AdS
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/1710.09557"} arXiv:1710.09557
          %h4#ReferencesApplicationToSelfDual2FormField Application to the 6d self-dual 2-form field
          %p
            A general discussion of Penrose-Ward-type transforms sending
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundles"} circle 2-bundles
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/holomorphic+line+2-bundles"}> holomorphic line 2-bundles
            ) on some twistor space to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/circle+2-bundles+with+connection"} circle 2-bundles with connection
            and self-dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/curvature"} curvature
            3-form on spacestime (expected to play a role in the description of the
            = succeed ")" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/6d+%282%2C0%29-superconformal+QFT"} 6d (2,0)-superconformal QFT
          %ul#MREIC11
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/David+Chatterjee"}> David Chatterjee
                , sections 4 and 8 of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em On gerbs
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://people.maths.ox.ac.uk/hitchin/hitchinstudents/chatterjee.pdf"} pdf
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Lionel+J.+Mason"}> Lionel J. Mason
                , R. A. Reid-Edwards, A. Taghavi-Chabert, appendix of
                = succeed "," do
                  %em Conformal field theories in six-dimensional twistor space
                = succeed ")" do
                  %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1111.2585"} arXiv:1111.2585
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"}> Christian Saemann
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Martin+Wolf"} Martin Wolf
              = succeed "," do
                %em On twistors and conformal field theories from six dimensions
              = succeed ")" do
                %a{:href => "http://arxiv.org/abs/1111.2539"} arXiv.1111.2539
          %p
            More generally, there are arguments that the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/worldvolume"} worldvolume
            theory of several coincident
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/M5-branes"} M5-branes
            carries not just an abelian but a nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/higher+gauge+field"} higher gauge field
            given by a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundle"} principal 2-bundle
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-connection"} principal 2-connection
          %p
            The idea of generalizing the Penrose-Ward transform to one that takes nonabelian
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-bundles"} principal 2-bundles
            to
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/self-dual+higher+gauge+field"} self-dual
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/principal+2-connections"} principal 2-connections
            is explored in
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Christian+Saemann"}> Christian Saemann
